     using the appropriate syntax.

     If you follow the PKIX recommendations and just use one OID, then you
     just include the value of that OID.  Multiple OIDs can be set separated
     by commas, for example:

           certificatePolicies= 1.2.4.5, 1.1.3.4

     If you wish to include qualifiers, then the policy OID and qualifiers
     need to be specified in a separate section: this is done by using the
     @_s_e_c_t_i_o_n syntax instead of a literal OID value.

     The section referred to must include the policy OID using the name
     ppoolliiccyyIIddeennttiiffiieerr.  CCPPSSuurrii qualifiers can be included using the syntax:

           CCPPSS._n_n_n=_v_a_l_u_e

     uusseerrNNoottiiccee qualifiers can be set using the syntax:

           uusseerrNNoottiiccee._n_n_n=@_n_o_t_i_c_e

     The value of the uusseerrNNoottiiccee qualifier is specified in the relevant sec‐
     tion.  This section can include eexxpplliicciittTTeexxtt, oorrggaanniizzaattiioonn, and
     nnoottiicceeNNuummbbeerrss options.  eexxpplliicciittTTeexxtt and oorrggaanniizzaattiioonn are text strings,
     and nnoottiicceeNNuummbbeerrss is a comma separated list of numbers.  The oorrggaanniizzaattiioonn
     and nnoottiicceeNNuummbbeerrss options (if included) must _b_o_t_h be present.  If you use
     the uusseerrNNoottiiccee option with IE5 then you need the iiaa55oorrgg option at the top
     level to modify the encoding: otherwise it will not be interpreted prop‐
     erly.  Example:

           certificatePolicies=ia5org,1.2.3.4,1.5.6.7.8,@polsect

           [polsect]
           policyIdentifier = 1.3.5.8
